insert into toolbox_talks(code, category, title, summary, points, discuss, minutes, sort) values
  ($q$tt01$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Slips, Trips and Falls$q$, $q$The most common cause of injury at work is also one of the easiest to prevent.$q$, array[$q$Clean up spills straight away, or put up a wet floor sign and report it until it is dealt with.$q$, $q$Keep walkways, corridors and stairs clear. Do not leave boxes, cables or bags where people walk.$q$, $q$Wear sensible footwear with good grip, and take extra care when floors are wet, such as on rainy days.$q$, $q$Report loose carpet, damaged flooring, broken handrails and poor lighting rather than working around them.$q$, $q$Do not carry loads you cannot see over. Use the handrail on stairs and never run.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where on our site is the floor most likely to be wet or cluttered?$q$, $q$What is one thing we could change this week to make a walkway safer?$q$]::text[], 8, 1),
  ($q$tt02$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Manual Handling$q$, $q$Lifting and carrying causes a lot of back and joint injuries. Planning the job protects you.$q$, array[$q$Think before you lift: avoid it if you can, use a trolley or ask for help, and reduce the load if you cannot avoid it.$q$, $q$Check the Task, the Individual, the Load and the Environment: how far, how heavy, how awkward, and is the route clear?$q$, $q$Keep the load close to your body, bend your knees, keep your back straight and avoid twisting.$q$, $q$Break up large or heavy loads where possible and share the lift with a colleague, agreeing who leads.$q$, $q$Do not lift if you are injured or unwell, and report any pain or strain early.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What do we lift or carry most often here, and could we use equipment instead?$q$, $q$How do you let a colleague know you need a hand?$q$]::text[], 10, 2),
  ($q$tt03$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Working at Height$q$, $q$A fall from a ladder or step can be serious, even from a low height.$q$, array[$q$Avoid working at height if the job can be done another way, for example with an extension pole.$q$, $q$Use the right equipment for the job. Ladders are for short, low-risk tasks, not long jobs.$q$, $q$Check ladders and steps before each use: feet, rungs, locks and condition. Never use damaged equipment.$q$, $q$Keep three points of contact, do not overreach, and never stand on the top step.$q$, $q$Set the equipment on firm, level ground, and keep other people away from the area below.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What jobs here involve going up a ladder or steps?$q$, $q$Who checks that our ladders are in good condition, and how often?$q$]::text[], 12, 3),
  ($q$tt04$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Electrical Safety at Work$q$, $q$Most electrical accidents come from damaged equipment or misuse. A quick look before use makes a difference.$q$, array[$q$Look at plugs, cables and equipment before you use them. Do not use anything damaged, hot or burning-smelling.$q$, $q$Do not overload sockets or daisy-chain extension leads. Unroll extension reels fully.$q$, $q$Keep liquids away from electrical equipment, and never touch electrics with wet hands.$q$, $q$Do not attempt repairs yourself. Switch off, unplug if it is safe, label it as faulty and report it.$q$, $q$Portable appliances are usually tested and labelled at intervals, and fixed wiring is inspected periodically. Report anything that looks overdue.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Do we have anywhere that looks overloaded or has trailing cables?$q$, $q$What would you do if you saw sparks from a socket?$q$]::text[], 10, 4),
  ($q$tt05$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Using Work Equipment Safely$q$, $q$Machines and tools are safe when they are used as intended, by trained people, with guards in place.$q$, array[$q$Only use equipment you have been trained and authorised to use.$q$, $q$Read the instructions and follow them, and use guards and safety devices. Never remove or bypass them.$q$, $q$Check equipment before use, and report faults at once. Take faulty equipment out of use and label it.$q$, $q$Isolate equipment from the power before cleaning, clearing a jam or adjusting it.$q$, $q$Keep long hair, loose clothing and jewellery away from moving parts.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What equipment here are you not fully confident using?$q$, $q$How do we tag out equipment that is faulty?$q$]::text[], 10, 5),
  ($q$tt06$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Hazardous Substances (COSHH)$q$, $q$Cleaning products, paints, solvents and dusts can harm health if they are not used and stored properly.$q$, array[$q$Look at the label and the safety data sheet before you use a product, and follow what it says.$q$, $q$Learn the hazard symbols. They show if a product is corrosive, flammable, toxic or irritating.$q$, $q$Keep products in their original, labelled containers. Never decant into drinks bottles or unlabelled containers.$q$, $q$Wear the protective equipment the sheet asks for, wash your hands afterwards, and never mix products.$q$, $q$Store products securely and report spills, leaks or any symptoms such as skin irritation or breathing problems.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where are the safety data sheets kept on this site?$q$, $q$Which product here would be most harmful if it were used the wrong way?$q$]::text[], 12, 6),
  ($q$tt07$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Reporting Accidents and Near Misses$q$, $q$Reporting what went wrong, or nearly did, is how we stop it happening to someone else.$q$, array[$q$Report every accident, injury and near miss, however small it seems, as soon as possible.$q$, $q$Record it in the accident book or the way your workplace asks, with facts: what, where, when and who.$q$, $q$Near misses are free lessons. A trip that did not cause an injury this time might next time.$q$, $q$Reporting is about fixing the problem, not blaming the person.$q$, $q$Some serious incidents and illnesses must be reported to the HSE by your employer. Tell your manager straight away.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Do you know where the accident book is kept?$q$, $q$What near miss have you seen that nobody reported?$q$]::text[], 8, 7),
  ($q$tt08$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)$q$, $q$PPE is the last line of defence. It only works when it is the right kind, fitted well and worn.$q$, array[$q$PPE is used when other ways of controlling a risk are not enough. It does not remove the hazard.$q$, $q$Your employer must provide the right PPE free of charge, and you must wear it as instructed.$q$, $q$Check it before each use. Damaged, dirty or out-of-date PPE should be replaced, not used.$q$, $q$Make sure it fits. Poorly fitting gloves, masks or boots can cause as many problems as they solve.$q$, $q$Store it clean and dry, and tell your manager if you need a different size or type.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What PPE do we need for the jobs we do here?$q$, $q$What makes PPE uncomfortable, and what could we do about it?$q$]::text[], 8, 8),
  ($q$tt09$q$, $q$Health & Safety$q$, $q$Lone Working$q$, $q$Working alone is not unsafe by itself, but help is further away if something goes wrong.$q$, array[$q$Make sure someone knows where you are, what you are doing and when you will finish.$q$, $q$Agree a check-in routine, and what happens if you do not check in on time.$q$, $q$Carry a charged phone and know how to call for help. Check you have signal where you are working.$q$, $q$Never take a risk because nobody is watching. Stop if the job is not safe to do alone.$q$, $q$Report any threat, aggressive visitor or feeling of being unsafe, and know your escape route.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Who here works alone, and when?$q$, $q$How would we know if a lone worker needed help?$q$]::text[], 8, 9),
  ($q$tt10$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$Preventing Fires$q$, $q$Most fires start from something simple that could have been stopped. Prevention is everyone’s job.$q$, array[$q$Keep ignition sources away from anything that burns. Check heaters, cookers and electrical equipment, and switch off at the end of the day.$q$, $q$Do not overload sockets, and report hot, damaged or burning-smelling equipment.$q$, $q$Keep waste and packaging away from buildings and electrics. Empty bins regularly.$q$, $q$Store flammable liquids and gases properly, in small quantities, away from heat.$q$, $q$Follow the smoking rules, and make sure doors and windows are secured at closing time to reduce the risk of arson.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What is the most likely cause of a fire on this site?$q$, $q$What do we do at the end of the day to reduce the risk?$q$]::text[], 10, 10),
  ($q$tt11$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$If You Discover a Fire$q$, $q$Knowing what to do before it happens saves time and lives.$q$, array[$q$Raise the alarm straight away by shouting a warning and using the nearest call point.$q$, $q$Call 999 and ask for the fire and rescue service. Give the address and say what is on fire.$q$, $q$Only tackle a very small fire if you are trained, it is safe to do so, and you have a clear escape route behind you.$q$, $q$Leave by the nearest safe exit and close doors behind you if you can. Do not use lifts.$q$, $q$Go to the assembly point, report to whoever is taking the roll call, and do not go back in for any reason.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where is the nearest alarm call point to where you work?$q$, $q$What is the sound of our fire alarm, and what do you do when you hear it?$q$]::text[], 10, 11),
  ($q$tt12$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$Fire Exits, Escape Routes and Assembly Points$q$, $q$A fire exit only helps if people can get to it, open it, and know where to go next.$q$, array[$q$Keep fire exits and escape routes clear at all times. Never store anything in corridors, on stairs or in front of doors.$q$, $q$Exit doors must open easily from the inside without a key while people are in the building.$q$, $q$Know at least two ways out of where you work, and the way to the assembly point.$q$, $q$Report blocked exits, missing or unlit signs, and anything that would slow people down.$q$, $q$Tell your manager if someone you work with, or a regular visitor, would need help to get out.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Can everyone here name the assembly point?$q$, $q$What is the one thing most likely to block our exits?$q$]::text[], 8, 12),
  ($q$tt13$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$Fire Extinguishers: Know Your Types$q$, $q$The wrong extinguisher on the wrong fire can make things worse. Know what is on your site.$q$, array[$q$Extinguishers have a red body with a colour band showing the type: water, foam, powder, carbon dioxide or wet chemical.$q$, $q$Water is for paper, wood and fabric. It must never be used on electrical fires or burning fat or oil.$q$, $q$Carbon dioxide is commonly used for electrical fires. Wet chemical is made for cooking oils and fats.$q$, $q$Only use one if you have been trained, the fire is small and you can leave quickly. If in doubt, get out.$q$, $q$Report extinguishers that are missing, blocked, damaged or past their service date.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What types of extinguisher do we have, and where are they?$q$, $q$Which fire here would need which type?$q$]::text[], 8, 13),
  ($q$tt14$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$Fire Alarms and Emergency Lighting$q$, $q$Alarms and emergency lights are tested regularly. You can help by noticing when something is not right.$q$, array[$q$The alarm is commonly tested weekly, and emergency lights are commonly checked monthly. Know when this happens at your site.$q$, $q$Do not cover, block or interfere with smoke detectors, call points or sounders.$q$, $q$Report any alarm that does not sound, a panel showing a fault, or an emergency light that is out or flickering.$q$, $q$Never ignore an alarm because you think it is a test or a false alarm. Treat every alarm as real unless you have been told otherwise.$q$, $q$Take part in drills and learn what the signs and symbols mean.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Who tests the alarm here, and where is the record kept?$q$, $q$Have you seen an emergency light that did not look right?$q$]::text[], 8, 14),
  ($q$tt15$q$, $q$Fire Safety$q$, $q$Fire Doors: Keep Them Shut$q$, $q$A closed fire door can hold back fire and smoke for long enough for people to escape.$q$, array[$q$Fire doors only work if they are closed. Never wedge or prop one open.$q$, $q$If a door is held open, it should use an approved hold-open device that releases when the alarm sounds.$q$, $q$Check the door closes fully by itself, the seals and gaps look right, and the closer is working.$q$, $q$Do not damage doors or glass, and do not remove or paint over seals.$q$, $q$Report damaged, sticking or missing-closer fire doors at once.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Which doors here are fire doors?$q$, $q$What stops people from closing them, and what could we do about it?$q$]::text[], 8, 15),
  ($q$tt16$q$, $q$Wellbeing$q$, $q$Stress and Mental Wellbeing$q$, $q$Everyone has pressure sometimes. Spotting it early and talking about it helps.$q$, array[$q$Stress shows up differently for different people: tiredness, irritability, trouble concentrating, or avoiding work.$q$, $q$Talking helps. Speak to your manager, a colleague you trust, or someone outside work.$q$, $q$Managers should listen, look at workload and what can be changed, and follow up.$q$, $q$Look after the basics: breaks, food, water, sleep and time away from work.$q$, $q$If you are struggling, help is available. The Samaritans are free on 116 123, day or night.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What is one thing that makes work harder than it needs to be?$q$, $q$How can we make it easier to say when we are struggling?$q$]::text[], 10, 16),
  ($q$tt17$q$, $q$Wellbeing$q$, $q$Working in Hot and Cold Conditions$q$, $q$Temperature affects how we feel and how safely we work.$q$, array[$q$In the heat: drink water regularly, take breaks in the shade or somewhere cool, and wear light, breathable clothing.$q$, $q$Signs of heat illness include headache, dizziness, nausea and heavy sweating. Stop, cool down and get help.$q$, $q$In the cold: wear layers, keep hands and feet warm, and take warm drinks and breaks.$q$, $q$Take extra care on icy ground and in poor weather. Report ice, flooding or leaks.$q$, $q$Tell your manager if your workplace is regularly too hot or too cold to work comfortably.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where on this site do people struggle with the temperature?$q$, $q$What do we do on the hottest and coldest days?$q$]::text[], 8, 17),
  ($q$tt18$q$, $q$Wellbeing$q$, $q$Display Screen Equipment$q$, $q$Setting up your workstation properly prevents aches, eye strain and long-term problems.$q$, array[$q$Sit with your back supported, feet flat, and elbows level with the desk.$q$, $q$Place the screen at arm’s length with the top of the screen around eye level.$q$, $q$Take short, frequent breaks from the screen. A few minutes away every hour helps.$q$, $q$Keep your keyboard and mouse close, wrists straight, and the desk clear for room to move.$q$, $q$Tell your manager about pain, tingling or eye strain. You can ask for a workstation assessment.$q$]::text[], array[$q$How is your own desk set up right now?$q$, $q$What would make your workstation more comfortable?$q$]::text[], 8, 18),
  ($q$tt19$q$, $q$Wellbeing$q$, $q$Tiredness and Fatigue$q$, $q$Being tired makes mistakes, accidents and bad decisions more likely.$q$, array[$q$Tiredness slows reactions and makes it harder to concentrate, so it increases risk, especially driving, using equipment and working at height.$q$, $q$Signs include yawning, heavy eyes, mistakes, and drifting off. Take them seriously.$q$, $q$Caffeine is not a fix. Rest, food, water and a short break help more.$q$, $q$Plan your sleep around shifts and early starts, and avoid long hours back to back where you can.$q$, $q$Tell your manager if you are too tired to work safely or to drive home.$q$]::text[], array[$q$When are you most tired at work?$q$, $q$What would you do if a colleague looked too tired to carry on safely?$q$]::text[], 8, 19),
  ($q$tt20$q$, $q$Wellbeing$q$, $q$Good Hygiene and Preventing Infection$q$, $q$Simple habits stop germs spreading between colleagues, customers and visitors.$q$, array[$q$Wash your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds: after the toilet, before eating, after handling waste and when they are dirty.$q$, $q$Cover coughs and sneezes with a tissue, bin it and wash your hands.$q$, $q$Clean regularly touched surfaces such as door handles, switches, taps and shared equipment.$q$, $q$Cover cuts and grazes with a waterproof dressing.$q$, $q$If you have had sickness or diarrhoea, stay away until you have been clear for 48 hours, and tell your manager.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where are the handwashing facilities here, and are they well stocked?$q$, $q$What are the most touched surfaces in our workplace?$q$]::text[], 8, 20),
  ($q$tt21$q$, $q$Workplace Responsibilities$q$, $q$Health and Safety: Everyone’s Responsibility$q$, $q$Employers have duties, and so do the people who work for them.$q$, array[$q$Your employer must provide a safe place to work, safe equipment, training, and information about the risks.$q$, $q$You must take reasonable care of your own safety and of others affected by what you do.$q$, $q$You must cooperate with your employer, follow instructions and training, and use equipment as intended.$q$, $q$You must not interfere with or misuse anything provided for health and safety.$q$, $q$You can, and should, speak up about anything that looks unsafe.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What is one safety rule here that people sometimes skip?$q$, $q$Who do you tell when you spot a hazard?$q$]::text[], 10, 21),
  ($q$tt22$q$, $q$Workplace Responsibilities$q$, $q$First Aid: Who and Where$q$, $q$In an emergency, the first few minutes matter. Know who can help and where the equipment is.$q$, array[$q$Know who the first aiders or appointed persons are, and how to reach them quickly.$q$, $q$Know where the first aid kit is, and tell someone when items are used or out of date.$q$, $q$In a serious emergency, call 999 first, then fetch help.$q$, $q$Do not give medication unless you are trained and authorised. Stay with the person until help arrives.$q$, $q$Record every incident that needs first aid, and tell your manager.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Who are our first aiders, and where is the first aid kit?$q$, $q$What would you do if a colleague collapsed in front of you?$q$]::text[], 8, 22),
  ($q$tt23$q$, $q$Workplace Responsibilities$q$, $q$Visitors and Contractors on Site$q$, $q$People who do not know the building need to be kept safe and need to keep our site safe.$q$, array[$q$Everyone signs in and out, so we know who is on site if there is an emergency.$q$, $q$Brief visitors and contractors on the fire alarm, exits and assembly point.$q$, $q$Contractors should have a clear scope of work, and where needed a permit to work for jobs such as hot work or roof access.$q$, $q$Check the asbestos register and site information with contractors before any work that could disturb the building.$q$, $q$Escort visitors if they are not familiar with the site, and challenge politely anyone who is not signed in.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What do visitors need to know when they arrive here?$q$, $q$What would you do if you saw a stranger in a staff area?$q$]::text[], 8, 23),
  ($q$tt24$q$, $q$Workplace Responsibilities$q$, $q$Security and Locking Up$q$, $q$Good security protects people, stock and the building, and reduces the risk of fire.$q$, array[$q$Keep keys, fobs and alarm codes secure and do not share them. Report anything lost straight away.$q$, $q$At closing, check all areas, switch off non-essential equipment, close windows and fire doors and lock up.$q$, $q$Make sure nobody is left inside, especially in toilets, stores and back rooms.$q$, $q$Set the alarm as you have been trained, and know what to do if it activates.$q$, $q$Report suspicious people or activity and any damage, and never put yourself at risk.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What is the closing routine here, and is it written down?$q$, $q$What would you do if you found a door open in the morning?$q$]::text[], 8, 24),
  ($q$tt25$q$, $q$Workplace Responsibilities$q$, $q$Treating Each Other with Respect$q$, $q$A safe workplace is also one where people feel respected and able to speak up.$q$, array[$q$Everyone has the right to be treated with dignity. Bullying, harassment and discrimination are not acceptable.$q$, $q$Banter is only banter if everyone is comfortable with it. If it hurts, it is a problem.$q$, $q$If someone upsets you, tell them if you feel able to, or tell your manager. You will be taken seriously.$q$, $q$If you see it happening to someone else, you can support them and report it.$q$, $q$Know your workplace policy and who you can speak to confidentially.$q$]::text[], array[$q$What does a respectful team look like here?$q$, $q$Who would you go to if you needed to raise a concern?$q$]::text[], 10, 25),
  ($q$tt26$q$, $q$Industry Specific$q$, $q$Legionella and Water Safety$q$, $q$Legionella bacteria can grow in water systems that are not managed. It can cause serious illness.$q$, array[$q$The bacteria grow in water kept between about 20 and 45 degrees, especially where it is still.$q$, $q$Little-used taps and showers are flushed regularly (commonly weekly) so water does not stand still. Follow your site’s schedule and record it.$q$, $q$Water temperatures are checked and recorded. Hot water should be hot and cold water should be cold.$q$, $q$Do not remove, change or disinfect showerheads or taps unless you have been told to.$q$, $q$Report water that is discoloured, smelly, too warm or cold, and any leaks or dead ends in pipework.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Which taps or showers here are used least?$q$, $q$Where is our Legionella risk assessment, and who looks after it?$q$]::text[], 10, 26),
  ($q$tt27$q$, $q$Industry Specific$q$, $q$Asbestos Awareness$q$, $q$Buildings built before 2000 may contain asbestos. It is safe when it is undisturbed and dangerous when it is damaged.$q$, array[$q$Asbestos was used in many materials such as ceiling tiles, insulation, boards and pipe lagging.$q$, $q$Look at the asbestos register before any work that could drill into, cut or break the building.$q$, $q$If you are not sure whether a material contains asbestos, treat it as if it does. Stop work and report it.$q$, $q$Never sweep, brush or vacuum suspected debris. Keep other people away from the area.$q$, $q$Report any damage to materials, and never remove asbestos unless you are licensed and trained.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Do you know where the asbestos register is kept?$q$, $q$What work here could disturb the fabric of the building?$q$]::text[], 10, 27),
  ($q$tt28$q$, $q$Industry Specific$q$, $q$Gas Safety and Gas Leaks$q$, $q$Gas is safe when it is installed and maintained properly. Everyone should know what to do if something goes wrong.$q$, array[$q$If you smell gas: do not use switches, phones or flames. Open doors and windows and turn off at the meter if it is safe.$q$, $q$Leave the building and call the National Gas Emergency Service on 0800 111 999. It is free, and it is open at any time.$q$, $q$Only Gas Safe registered engineers should work on gas equipment.$q$, $q$Know the signs of carbon monoxide poisoning: headache, dizziness, nausea and tiredness. Alarms should be fitted where needed.$q$, $q$Report faulty gas appliances, yellow or lazy flames and sooty marks straight away.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where is the gas shut-off here?$q$, $q$When was gas equipment here last checked, and where is the record?$q$]::text[], 8, 28),
  ($q$tt29$q$, $q$Industry Specific$q$, $q$Emergency Shut-offs and Contacts$q$, $q$In an emergency, being able to isolate the gas, water or power quickly can prevent serious damage and harm.$q$, array[$q$Know where the gas, water and electric shut-offs are for your site, and who is allowed to use them.$q$, $q$Know where the assembly point is and who the key contacts are, such as keyholders and managers.$q$, $q$Keep this information up to date. It is stored under Emergency Information in your passport.$q$, $q$Never try to isolate anything if it is not safe to do so. Leave and call for help.$q$, $q$Put the numbers for emergency services, your keyholders and your contractors where they can be found quickly.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Can everyone here find the water, gas and electric shut-offs?$q$, $q$Is our contact list correct today?$q$]::text[], 8, 29),
  ($q$tt30$q$, $q$Industry Specific$q$, $q$Pest Control: Spotting and Reporting$q$, $q$Pests can damage property, spoil stock and spread disease. Early reporting makes control easier.$q$, array[$q$Signs include droppings, gnaw marks, nests, smears, noises and sightings. Report anything you see.$q$, $q$Keep food and waste in sealed containers and clean up spills and crumbs.$q$, $q$Keep doors and windows closed and report gaps, holes and damaged seals around pipes and doors.$q$, $q$Do not put down poison or traps yourself. Leave it to your pest control contractor.$q$, $q$Keep a record of sightings and visits, and share it with the contractor.$q$]::text[], array[$q$Where on this site would pests be most likely to get in?$q$, $q$Who do we report a sighting to?$q$]::text[], 8, 30);
